    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "COSO Principle 13 requires the organization to obtain or generate and use relevant, quality information to support the functioning of internal control. Information quality is defined by fitness for purpose — data must be accurate, complete, timely, and usable for the specific decisions and reporting processes it supports. The CIO's claim that migration was 'technically complete' because data was loaded into the ERP confuses data movement with data quality. COSO Principle 13 does not require that data be loaded into a system — it requires that the information used in internal control and financial reporting be of sufficient quality to support reliable decision-making and reporting. The four failures (unit-of-measure mismatch, customer duplication, omitted WIP records, and absence of proactive communication to the financial reporting team) all demonstrate that the migrated data was not fit for the purpose of financial reporting. The $4.7 million unexplained variance is the consequence. In practice, acquisition integration must include a data quality validation step where migrated data is tested for completeness (are all records present?), accuracy (do values mean the same thing in the new system?), and usability (can the financial reporting team actually use the data to close the books?). The business interpretation is that 'loading data' and 'delivering quality information' are distinct control objectives — COSO Principle 13 is about the latter.",
